pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib9"><citation citation-type="journal">Brummendorf, T., and V. Lemmon. <year>2001</year>. Immunoglobulin superfamily receptors: cis-interactions, intracellular adapters and alternative splicing regulate adhesion. <source>Curr. Opin. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>611</fpage>&#x02013;618.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11544031</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal">Bruning, A., and I.B. Runnebaum. <year>2004</year>. The coxsackie adenovirus receptor inhibits cancer cell migration. <source>Exp. Cell Res.</source> <volume>298</volume>:<fpage>624</fpage>&#x02013;631.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15265708</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Campbell, J.A., P. Schelling, J.D. Wetzel, E.M. Johnson, J.C. Forrest, G.A.R. Wilson, M. Aurrand-Lions, B.A. Imhof, T. Stehle, and T.S. Dermody. <year>2005</year>. Junctional adhesion molecule A serves as a receptor for prototype and field-isolate strains of mammalian reovirus. <source>J. Virol.</source> <volume>79</volume>:<fpage>7967</fpage>&#x02013;7978.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15956543</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Carson, S.D., J.T. Hobbs, S.M. Tracy, and N.M. Chapman. <year>1999</year>. Expression of the coxsackievirus and adenovirus receptor in cultured human umbilical vein endothelial cells: regulation in response to cell density. <source>J. Virol.</source> <volume>73</volume>:<fpage>7077</fpage>&#x02013;7079.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10400813</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Chavakis, T., T. Keiper, R. Matz-Westphal, K. Hersemeyer, U.J. Sachs, P.P. Nawroth, K.T. Preissner, and S. Santoso. <year>2004</year>. The junctional adhesion molecule-C promotes neutrophil transendothelial migration <italic>in vitro</italic> and <italic>in vivo.</italic> <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>279</volume>:<fpage>55602</fpage>&#x0201